00:10:46.334 --> 00:10:52.758
all these forces brought together along with contributions from turbo machinery

144
00:10:52.758 --> 00:10:57.978
and from external aerodynamics contributors, all this has led to

145
00:10:58.178 --> 00:11:02.831
to through its bench. So the important point here is, well,

146
00:11:02.831 --> 00:11:08.880
I would say the rationale between this effort is that we hear very frequently

147
00:11:08.880 --> 00:11:15.395
that surrogates aim to replace CFD, right? And the problem is that surrogate models

148
00:11:15.395 --> 00:11:17.178
are often assessed only

149
00:11:17.418 --> 00:11:22.532
on global error metrics, and which is not what we do in CFD.

150
00:11:22.532 --> 00:11:27.227
Nobody would assess, say that the CFD code is good just

151
00:11:27.227 --> 00:11:32.844
because it has a low error on the velocity field on average. Okay,

152
00:11:32.844 --> 00:11:37.538
so if we aim at replacing CFD with surrogate models, we

153
00:11:37.618 --> 00:11:42.620
should assess them as severely as CFD. So the first question is about

154
00:11:42.620 --> 00:11:45.979
convergence. So when you run a CFD simulation,

155
00:11:45.979 --> 00:11:51.053
what you do first of all is that you cheque if your mesh is converged,

156
00:11:51.053 --> 00:11:56.698
if your iterations are converged and so on. So we should do something similar.

157
00:11:56.898 --> 00:12:02.354
with surrogate models and see if the model has converged to some extent in

158
00:12:02.354 --> 00:12:05.554
terms, for instance, of data we assimilate.

159
00:12:05.554 --> 00:12:10.427
The second very important point is the flow quantities we look at,

160
00:12:10.427 --> 00:12:15.810
not only L2 errors on global fields, especially in external aerodynamics,

161
00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:21.338
where most of the field is basically a constant function. So we should look

162
00:12:21.538 --> 00:12:24.279
like profiles, world distributions and forces.

163
00:12:24.279 --> 00:12:28.477
This is what we are proposing here. And another important point are the

164
00:12:28.477 --> 00:12:31.509
derivatives, because reconstructing well a velocity

165
00:12:31.509 --> 00:12:35.532
field doesn't mean that you are reconstructing well the derivatives.

166
00:12:35.532 --> 00:12:39.905
The derivatives are much harder to reconstruct. And however, for instance,

167
00:12:39.905 --> 00:12:43.578
velocity derivatives or temperature derivatives will influence

168
00:12:43.858 --> 00:12:48.142
your wall shear stress, your heat fluxes, and the separation,

169
00:12:48.142 --> 00:12:53.669
the separation and reattachment points. So this is very important and we should

170
00:12:53.669 --> 00:12:57.262
look at those quantities more closely. And finally,

171
00:12:57.262 --> 00:13:01.960
the last point is overall cost, because when you train a surrogate,

172
00:13:01.960 --> 00:13:07.418
the idea is that you replace costly CFD with a cheap surrogate where inference

173
00:13:07.658 --> 00:13:13.071
takes only a few seconds, okay? But imagine that you are addressing an

174
00:13:13.071 --> 00:13:17.263
optimization task, and this optimization task could be

175
00:13:17.263 --> 00:13:21.151
resolved with brute force CFD using, I don't know,

176
00:13:21.151 --> 00:13:26.183
1000 queries to the costly CFD code. And to train your surrogate,

177
00:13:26.183 --> 00:13:28.698
you need a database of 10,000 CFD

178
00:13:28.978 --> 00:13:34.486
So then actually you have gained very little in terms of computational cost,

179
00:13:34.486 --> 00:13:37.847
or you have even lost some computational cost.

180
00:13:37.847 --> 00:13:43.211
So we should be careful and look very, very carefully at these quantities.

181
00:13:43.211 --> 00:13:48.218
And that's what we do using open evaluations, submissions reviewed by

