200
00:14:50.458 --> 00:14:55.307
Hi everyone, I'm Jean Kossaifi. I lead the AI engineering group at NVIDIA

201
00:14:55.307 --> 00:14:59.567
Research. And so, yeah, I want to talk a little bit about how we

202
00:14:59.567 --> 00:15:05.071
are measuring the progress in the field. And so, in general, in engineering design,

203
00:15:05.071 --> 00:15:09.658
we basically want to predict how a system, our geometry, will behave.

204
00:15:09.978 --> 00:15:14.818
and determine how to improve it. And so one of the main goals of AI

205
00:15:14.818 --> 00:15:20.298
engineering is to use lead surrogate models to make those predictions faster

206
00:15:20.298 --> 00:15:24.142
and enable faster, large-scale design exploration and

207
00:15:24.142 --> 00:15:29.978
optimization. And so for car aerodynamics, for example, we need to understand how

208
00:15:30.178 --> 00:15:34.618
When we change the geometry that affects the flow and the forces, and...

209
00:15:35.578 --> 00:15:39.299
do the simulation across several operating conditions.

210
00:15:39.299 --> 00:15:44.238
And so the more we want to optimise, the more evaluations we need to do.

211
00:15:44.238 --> 00:15:49.785
And so CFD already offers a whole range of established approaches from, you know,

212
00:15:49.785 --> 00:15:54.995
RANs to hybrid RANs, LES, LES, DNS. And so each of those differ in the types

213
00:15:54.995 --> 00:15:56.618
of physics they resolve.

214
00:15:56.938 --> 00:16:01.510
the physics, the model, and the computational cost. And right now,

215
00:16:01.510 --> 00:16:07.105
even simulations we can afford for a few configurations might be too expensive to

216
00:16:07.105 --> 00:16:12.905
run through the entire optimization loop. And so really, the bottleneck, the budget,

217
00:16:12.905 --> 00:16:15.498
whether it's both in time and compute,

218
00:16:15.818 --> 00:16:20.612
is currently limiting the number of designs we can explore and the fidelity

219
00:16:20.612 --> 00:16:24.459
of the solvers we can use. And so learning a surrogate model

220
00:16:24.459 --> 00:16:29.505
essentially allows us to amortise the cost of simulation of data generation and

221
00:16:29.505 --> 00:16:34.361
model training over many evaluations. So we generate a lot of reference data

222
00:16:34.361 --> 00:16:37.578
and then we train a surrogate model from this data

223
00:16:38.058 --> 00:16:42.538
and we reuse the model that we trained once to make predictions across many

224
00:16:42.538 --> 00:16:44.778
geometries, operating conditions, etc.

225
00:16:45.778 --> 00:16:50.387
And so the hope is that we can bring information from the higher fidelity

226
00:16:50.387 --> 00:16:54.623
simulation into larger design study at a lower cost per prediction.

227
00:16:54.623 --> 00:16:57.426
And so really in aided engineering research,

228
00:16:57.426 --> 00:17:02.222
we're trying to develop neural operators that connect directly the geometry,

229
00:17:02.222 --> 00:17:05.336
the match, the representation directly to physics

230
00:17:05.336 --> 00:17:07.578
prediction and design optimizations.

231
00:17:08.538 --> 00:17:12.137
And so the reason we call it AI-aided engineering,

232
00:17:12.137 --> 00:17:17.146
and not let's say AI engineering, is that the models are complementing

233
00:17:17.146 --> 00:17:21.450
existing CAE methods. We're not really in the stage where we

234
00:17:21.450 --> 00:17:24.554
want to replace it. One issue, for example,

235
00:17:24.554 --> 00:17:28.858
is that the optimizer could exploit errors in the surrogate.

236
00:17:29.338 --> 00:17:33.099
So that find areas where it's out of distribution,

237
00:17:33.099 --> 00:17:37.818
so that a predictive design still needs to be checked with CFD.

238
00:17:38.778 --> 00:17:41.959
And so we still need experimental validation to assess how well the

239
00:17:41.959 --> 00:17:44.298
modelling actually represents the physics system.

240
00:17:45.258 --> 00:17:49.663
And so the predictions basically also need to support the design decisions

241
00:17:49.663 --> 00:17:54.244
we're making. If we're optimising drag, we want the model to predict how drag

242
00:17:54.244 --> 00:17:58.708
actually changes with the geometry. Small average errors in the flow fields

243
00:17:58.708 --> 00:18:02.701
don't necessarily tell you this. And so this is where the choice of

244
00:18:02.701 --> 00:18:04.698
metrics really matter. And CFD has

245
00:18:04.818 --> 00:18:08.826
Paola already said has a long history of benchmarking and validation.

246
00:18:08.826 --> 00:18:12.204
In other fields, Faez mentioned benchmark really have been

247
00:18:12.204 --> 00:18:15.410
driving progress. If you think, for example, in vision,

248
00:18:15.410 --> 00:18:17.929
ImageNet really is what provided, you know,

249
00:18:17.929 --> 00:18:22.738
common ground and training data for image recognition and was really central to the

250
00:18:22.738 --> 00:18:25.658
progress of AI and arriving to where we are today.

251
00:18:25.978 --> 00:18:29.380
So these benchmarks really help researchers compare methods,

252
00:18:29.380 --> 00:18:32.781
identify limitations, and decide which approaches to pursue.

253
00:18:32.781 --> 00:18:37.131
And so that's why we really put all our thoughts into designing fluids bench.

254
00:18:37.131 --> 00:18:40.254
And so there are two main questions we want to address,

255
00:18:40.254 --> 00:18:44.938
what the evaluation measures and whether the results are comparable across methods.

256
00:18:46.138 --> 00:18:51.655
So to start with what we're actually measuring, as we all know,

257
00:18:51.655 --> 00:18:58.552
a model can have low average field error and still predict drag or lift poorly.

258
00:18:58.552 --> 00:19:04.069
And so if you use that, those flow fields to optimise the cache

259
00:19:04.069 --> 00:19:06.138
shape, for example, then

260
00:19:06.618 --> 00:19:10.584
you really want to be looking at the drag error directly. Conversely,

261
00:19:10.584 --> 00:19:14.719
if you have a good drag prediction, that can result from cancellation of

262
00:19:14.719 --> 00:19:17.665
local errors, and it doesn't tell you that the flow

263
00:19:17.665 --> 00:19:21.460
field is actually accurate. So we need complementary measurements.

264
00:19:21.460 --> 00:19:25.766
You want field force measures to agree with the reference across the entire

265
00:19:25.766 --> 00:19:28.315
domain. You want to take into account proper

266
00:19:28.315 --> 00:19:28.938
quadrature.

267
00:19:29.258 --> 00:19:33.938
You want to look then at integrated forces to measure aerodynamic loads, etc.

268
00:19:33.938 --> 00:19:38.498
And then you have things like pressure and weight profile that can help you

269
00:19:38.498 --> 00:19:41.018
identify and diagnose local discrepancies.

270
00:19:42.178 --> 00:19:46.400
And then we're doing machine learning. So you also want to look at things like

271
00:19:46.400 --> 00:19:50.142
data efficiency, generalisation, how does performance change with the

272
00:19:50.142 --> 00:19:53.509
amount of training data? Does the method work well on held-out

273
00:19:53.509 --> 00:19:57.357
cases similar to the training data? At what point do you start to see a

274
00:19:57.357 --> 00:20:00.243
domain shift? What happens on different geometries or

275
00:20:00.243 --> 00:20:01.098
flow conditions?

276
00:20:02.058 --> 00:20:05.994
So this is another aspect of the benchmark. And lastly,

277
00:20:05.994 --> 00:20:11.406
it's also important to keep track of the source of the data, both, you know,

278
00:20:11.406 --> 00:20:15.202
for credit, but also because if you train a surrogate

279
00:20:15.202 --> 00:20:18.856
on RANS data, you typically test it on RANS data as

280
00:20:18.856 --> 00:20:21.738
well. So you know you can reproduce that.

281
00:20:21.898 --> 00:20:26.522
But good performance on that runs data doesn't tell you that it will agree with

282
00:20:26.522 --> 00:20:30.048
DNS or even wind tunnel experiments. And so as we mentioned,

283
00:20:30.048 --> 00:20:34.326
scoring is especially important in the machine learning community because

284
00:20:34.326 --> 00:20:39.065
researchers then use the reported results to choose their methods and decide what

285
00:20:39.065 --> 00:20:41.782
to work on next. And so the choice of metrics,

286
00:20:41.782 --> 00:20:43.458
normalisation and aggregation

287
00:20:43.538 --> 00:20:47.713
affects which improvements receive attention and where the field goes.

288
00:20:47.713 --> 00:20:51.299
And so on the one hand, benchmark really can drive the field

289
00:20:51.299 --> 00:20:54.650
forward with scores that can summarise performance well.

290
00:20:54.650 --> 00:20:59.119
But then we need individual metric to see where the improvements come from,

291
00:20:59.119 --> 00:21:02.058
you know, better field prediction, market forces.

292
00:21:02.378 --> 00:21:07.414
etc. But on the other hand, some scores can be gained and it can hide

